theory.intro:
A **logarithmic scale** measures a quantity by its order of magnitude: each whole step up the scale means ten times as much, so small numbers describe an enormous range. Real scales built this way are the **Richter scale**, **pH** and **decibels**.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-0.9,0) -- (6.6,0);
\foreach \x in {0,1.9,3.8,5.7} \draw (\x,-0.18) -- (\x,0.18);
\node[above] at (0,0.2) {$m$};
\node[above] at (1.9,0.2) {$m+1$};
\node[above] at (3.8,0.2) {$m+2$};
\node[above] at (5.7,0.2) {$m+3$};
\node[below] at (0,-0.22) {$A$};
\node[below] at (1.9,-0.22) {$10A$};
\node[below] at (3.8,-0.22) {$100A$};
\node[below] at (5.7,-0.22) {$1000A$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0.2,-1.25) -- (1.7,-1.25) node[midway, below] {$\times 10$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (2.1,-1.25) -- (3.6,-1.25) node[midway, below] {$\times 10$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (4.0,-1.25) -- (5.5,-1.25) node[midway, below] {$\times 10$};
\node[left] at (-1.0,0.45) {magnitude};
\node[left] at (-1.0,-0.55) {value};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.question_text:
The column graph shows the Richter magnitude of five earthquakes recorded in 2022. How many times greater was the ground amplitude at Chile than at Fiji?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.7]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,9.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(13.5,0);
\foreach \y in {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} {\draw (-0.2,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,0) {$0$};
\draw (0.8,0) rectangle (2.3,7);
\draw (3.1,0) rectangle (4.6,5);
\draw (5.4,0) rectangle (6.9,6);
\draw (7.7,0) rectangle (9.2,3);
\draw (10.0,0) rectangle (11.5,4);
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1.55,0) {Chile};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (3.85,0) {Nepal};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (6.15,0) {Peru};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (8.45,0) {Fiji};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (10.75,0) {Crete};
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.4,4.5) {Magnitude};
\node[below] at (6.15,-1.0) {Earthquake};
\node at (6.15,9.1) {Richter magnitudes};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
